    {
      "question": "e-6565",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5be6a2033f998d2d6638f4417b9c747926a95bd437aa80c2c3d6710f9c5c0433",
      "new_sha256": "bdf1f3a32175f619a8c095961f087f36cb43af74f2f500a5d023f7db6b845706",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6565",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero.",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6565.json",
          "file_sha256": "01fc6ca1282d0d81627f87afd6bbbaaa6d0c016235bdd5bb8f6d06e4b634d31c"
        }
      ],
