      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6567",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061497/example-6567.json",
          "file_sha256": "504d238e2ecf4e89843b168a663f292052855b4eb62c6a949b32041830476a79"
        }
